# SYNOPSIS

```text
driver NAME
class CLASS
match KIND ID
[directive ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

Each driver's directory holds a `driver.manifest`, which tells devmgr(8) which devices the driver serves.
The build collects every one into bootfs's `etc/drivers`, and devmgr matches each device it finds against them as data.

A driver's manifest is directives, one a line; `#` and what follows it are a comment.
Several drivers may follow one another in one file, each starting at its `driver` line.

: `driver`
  The driver's program: bootfs's `bin/<name>`.
  It comes first.
: `class`
  Its function class, such as `input` or `audio`.
: `match`
  A rule the driver matches devices by.
  The kinds are `pci <vendor>:<device>`, `pci-class <cc>.<ss>` or `<cc>.<ss>.<pp>` with a programming interface, `acpi <hid>` and `acpi-compatible <cid>`, in hexadecimal where they are numbers.
  A driver needs one at least.
: `fallback`
  A generic driver: any other driver's match wins over its.
: `dma-bits`
  How many bits of address its device can reach by DMA, 12 to 64.
: `sd`
  A block driver's sd(3) letter, a capital: devmgr binds its disks at `/dev/sd`<letter>`0`, `1`, and so on.

devmgr matches in levels: exact ids first (a PCI vendor and device, an ACPI hardware id), then compatible ids in the order the device lists them, then classes, a programming interface before none, then the fallback drivers' rules in the same order.
The first level any driver matches at decides.
Two drivers matching at it is an error, and neither runs.

# EXAMPLES

```text
driver virtio-input
class input
match pci 1af4:1052
```

# FILES

: `drivers/**/driver.manifest`
  In the tree, each driver's.
: `etc/drivers`
  In bootfs, all of them, in path order.

# SEE ALSO

devmgr(8), manifest(6)

# DIAGNOSTICS

Errors name the file and the line, such as `'dma-bits' takes 12 to 64`; a driver with no `match` is refused as matching nothing.
